    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Khoe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">San peoples</a> lived on the Cape peninsula and traded with passing ships long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> landed in 1652 and began a permanent settlement.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and then the British ruled the city, which depended on enslaved labour until the 1830s and drew on people brought from Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> permanently in 1814.</li><li>Under apartheid whole communities were removed, most notably the more than 60,000 residents of District Six, and the city remains one of the most racially segregated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1510 the Portuguese admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> and sixty-four of his men were killed at the Battle of Salt River by the Goringhaiqua, who used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> trained to respond to whistles and shouts.</p></aside>
    </section>

