    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1488</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> reaches the Cape and names it the Cape of Storms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1510</span><span class="rf-text">The Goringhaiqua defeat a Portuguese force at the Battle of Salt River</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1652</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-riebeeck">Jan van Riebeeck</a> lands with employees of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> to found a permanent station</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1658</span><span class="rf-text">A first large group of 402 enslaved people is brought to the Cape</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1806</span><span class="rf-text">British forces take the Cape after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blaauwberg">Battle of Blaauwberg</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">District Six is declared a white-only area and its housing is demolished</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> speaks from the balcony of the City Hall hours after his release</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1994</span><span class="rf-text">The first democratic election is held on 27 April</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John II of Portugal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">John II of Portugal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IOANNES_QVARTVS_PORTVGALIAE_REX_(Kunsthistorisches_Museum).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cape Town stands on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a>, beneath Table Mountain, on the Cape Peninsula at the south-western extremity of the African continent. The earliest known remnants of human occupation in the region were found at Peers Cave in Fish Hoek in the late 1920s; radiocarbon dates for one sample, SAM-AP 4692, fall in the mid-Holocene, at 5448 and 5136 BCE on the most generous and conservative estimates. Archaeologists have established that the peninsula and the south-western coastline were inhabited by various Khoe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">San peoples</a>, who mostly lived a semi-nomadic life. They included independent Khoe farmers with large families, the ǁAmmaqua (Watermans), a small group of Khoe traders, the Sonqua line-fishermen, a San offshoot, and the Ubiqua, a San clan of herder-hunters inland. The German anthropologist Theophilus Hahn recorded the original name of the area as ǁHui ǃGais, a Khoe word meaning "where clouds gather", and the Khoi called Table Mountain Hoeri 'kwaggo, "sea mountain".</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> arrived in 1488 and named the point the Cape of Storms; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">John II of Portugal</a> later renamed it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> recorded a sighting in 1497, and in 1503 the Portuguese admiral António de Saldanha named the mountain Taboa do Cabo, "table of the cape". In 1510 a Portuguese force led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> was defeated at the Battle of Salt River by the Goringhaiqua, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> clan, and Almeida and sixty-four of his men were killed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel.jpg" alt="Simon van der Stel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel">Simon van der Stel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simon_van_der_Stel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pieter van Anraedt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ships stopped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a> long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settled. Maritime records show that at least 1070 ships stopped at the Cape on outward-bound journeys between 1600 and 1652, and French, Danish, Dutch, English and above all Portuguese ships traded tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and iron with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoikhoi</a> clans for fresh meat and other provisions. The ǁAmmaqua supplied meat, water, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>; between 1601 and 1608 they traded 1839 sheep and 149 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> to four ships. This trade led them to build a permanent trading settlement by the Camissa river.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>'s party of three vessels landed on 6 April 1652. It laid out vegetable gardens and orchards, the origin of the Company's Garden, and channelled water from the Camissa into canals. Work on the Castle of Good Hope began in 1666 and finished in 1679; it is the oldest building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-van-der-stel">Simon van der Stel</a>, governor from 1679, founded the Cape wine industry, and Huguenot settlers arrived in 1688. By 1754 the settlement had 5,510 Europeans and 6,729 slaves. The Cape Town Municipality was formed in 1840, with 20,016 people, of whom 10,560 were white. The first railway line opened in 1859, and between 1891 and 1901 the population more than doubled, from 67,000 to 171,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> overtook Cape Town as the country's largest city by the 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes.jpg" alt="Cecil Rhodes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cecil_John_Rhodes_(Cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Bassano, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> held the Cape from 1652. Its monopoly on trade barred any private trade, and it granted farms along the Liesbeek River to a few servants from 1657. In 1795, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> invaded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Company ruined, British forces took the Cape after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muizenberg">Battle of Muizenberg</a>. The Cape was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> hands again from 1803, and British forces occupied it once more in 1806 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blaauwberg">Battle of Blaauwberg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1814">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1814</a> ceded it permanently to the United Kingdom, which saw control of the Cape as key to its command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Cape Town became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, whose territory expanded through the 1800s, partly through wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xhosa-people">amaXhosa</a> on its eastern frontier. The Cape Town Legislative Council was established in 1834. The colony elected its first parliament in 1854 on the non-racial Cape Qualified Franchise and won responsible government, with its own prime minister, in 1872. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>, as prime minister, later restricted that franchise. In 1910 the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> made Cape Town the legislative capital. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>'s victory in 1948 the already limited voting rights of the Coloured community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a> were revoked. The first democratic election took place on 27 April 1994.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> gave their own names to the people they met, calling the pastoralists "Hottentots", the coastal shellfish-eaters "Strandlopers" and the hunter-gatherers "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">Bushmen</a>". The first Asian immigrants, from 1654, were banished to the Cape by the Batavian High Court; they helped to form the Cape Coloured and Cape Malay communities and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to the Cape. In 1658 the first large group of 402 enslaved people was brought to work the farms, from present-day Benin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and from then on non-European migrants outnumbered Europeans. Enslaved people were also imported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, and trading in slaves remained significant; in 1766 the enslaved people on the slaver Meermin mutinied. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> ended in the 1830s, over 5500 enslaved people in the city were freed, almost a third of its population, and a Muslim community went on to found the Bo-Kaap.</p><p>After 1806 the native inhabitants were made to declare a fixed residence and could not move between regions without written permission; the vagrancy and pass laws of 1809 were repealed in 1829. Before the middle of the 20th century Cape Town was one of the most racially integrated cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. In the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> 35.7% of the metropolitan municipality's residents spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> as a first language, 29.8% Xhosa and 28.4% English. The dish bobotie dates from the 17th century, and the Kaapse Klopse minstrel carnival is held each year on 2 January.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/group-areas-act.jpg" alt="Group Areas Act" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mass_Meeting_at_Durban_on_May_28,_1950_to_protest_against_Group_Area_Bill_and_Suppression_of_Communism_Bill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Division, Government of India, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first of the border conflicts between the Company's settlement and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe</a> began in 1659 to 1660, when Nommoa, also known as Doman, organised resistance among several Khoekhoe groups after Van Riebeeck granted farms along the Liesbeek, grazing land of the Khoe. The Convict Crisis of 1849 followed a British proposal to make the Cape a penal colony and brought substantial civil upheaval.</p><p>Segregation first appeared after an outbreak of bubonic plague that was blamed on native Africans, who were moved to two locations outside the city, one near the docks and one at Ndabeni, about six km east. Ndabeni began what later grew into the townships of the Cape Flats. Under apartheid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> of 1950 classified and segregated urban areas by race, and formerly multiracial suburbs were purged of residents or demolished. District Six was declared a whites-only area in 1965; all its housing was demolished and over 60,000 residents were forcibly removed, many to the Cape Flats. Black South Africans were expelled to Langa, the city's oldest township, and the Cape was made a "Coloured labour preference area", to the exclusion of Black Africans. The government tried for decades to remove the largely Xhosa squatter camps such as Crossroads, and in the last forced removal, in May and June 1986, an estimated 70,000 people were expelled from their homes. In June 1976 school students in Langa, Gugulethu and Nyanga held their own protests after the Soweto Uprising; school buildings were burnt and the police met them with force.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela.jpg" alt="Nelson Mandela" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nelson_Mandela_1994.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kingkongphoto &amp; www.celebrity-photos.com from Laurel, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several old places remain: the Castle of Good Hope, the oldest building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and the Company's Garden, the country's oldest park. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robben-island">Robben Island</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/table-bay">Table Bay</a>, a place of banishment and imprisonment for nearly 400 years and also a leper colony and a mental hospital, was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1999. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> served 18 of his 27 years of imprisonment there, as did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kgalema-motlanthe">Kgalema Motlanthe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-zuma">Jacob Zuma</a>. On 11 February 1990, hours after his release, Mandela spoke from the balcony of the City Hall in his first public speech in decades. The District Six Museum records the history of the Cape Coloured community and of District Six.</p><p>Cape Town is the legislative capital of South Africa and the seat of its Parliament, the capital of the Western Cape and the country's second-most populous city. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,740,026 people in the metropolitan municipality. Table Mountain National Park lies within the city boundaries, in the Cape Floristic Region. About 60% of the population live in townships and informal settlements far from the centre, and the legacy of apartheid's planning means that Cape Town remains one of the most racially segregated cities in South Africa.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Cape Town was inhabited by Khoe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">San peoples</a> before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> founded a station there in 1652. Enslaved and banished people were brought to it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took it permanently in 1814, and apartheid removed whole districts such as District Six. It is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s legislative capital.</em></p>

